            <ArticleTitle>Modified tension band wiring of medial malleolar ankle fractures.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>64-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Twenty-two displaced medial malleolar ankle fractures that were treated surgically using the modified tension band method of Cleak and Dawson were retrospectively reviewed at an average follow-up of 25 months. The technique involves the use of a screw to anchor a figure-of-eight wire. There were no malreductions and all fractures healed. Problems with the technique included technical errors with hardware placement, medial ankle pain, and asymptomatic wire migration. Despite this, modified tension band wiring remains an acceptable method for fixation of selected displaced medial malleolar fractures. It is especially suited for small fracture fragments and osteoporotic bone.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
